      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "192b4275-7bee-5f00-b2cb-975d538a7106",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: California, southeastern California, Yuma, Arizona, Calexico, Imperial Valley, San Joaquin River\n- Systems: Madera Canal, All-American Canal, Delta–Mendota Canal, Central Valley Project, Friant Dam, Imperial Dam, Brock Reservoir, Friant-Kern Canal\n\nFacts:\n- The Madera Canal is a 35.9-mile aqueduct in California.\n- The All-American Canal is an 80-mile aqueduct in southeastern California.\n- Imperial Dam on the Colorado River, about 30 miles northeast of Yuma, Arizona, diverts water into the All-American Canal.\n- The All-American Canal runs to just west of Calexico, California, before its last branch turns mostly north into the Imperial Valley.\n- The Delta–Mendota Canal is part of the Central Valley Project.\n- The Delta–Mendota Canal is intended to replace water in the San Joaquin River that is diverted into the Madera Canal and Friant-Kern Canal at Friant Dam.\n- Brock Reservoir holds water from the All-American Canal until it is needed for nearby agriculture.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "ca68fdfe-35fc-5b92-818a-0fc7f8cad5a0",
        "text": "Page: Madera Canal. Sentence: The Madera Canal is a 35.9 mi aqueduct in the U.S. state of California.\nPage: All-American Canal. Sentence: The All-American Canal is an 80-mile (130 km) long aqueduct, located in southeastern California.\nPage: All-American Canal. Sentence: The Imperial Dam, about 30 miles (48 km) northeast of Yuma, Arizona on the Colorado River, diverts water into the All-American Canal, which runs to just west of Calexico, California before its last branch heads mostly north into the Imperial Valley.\nPage: Delta–Mendota Canal. Sentence: It is part of the Central Valley Project and its purpose is to replace water in the San Joaquin River that is diverted into Madera Canal and Friant-Kern Canal at Friant Dam.\nPage: Brock Reservoir. Sentence: It holds water from the All-American Canal until it can be used for nearby agriculture.",
